  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/aphyosemion-celiae",
      "html": "<p>Celia's lyretail, Aphyosemion celiae, is a killifish from western Cameroon, where it appears restricted to parts of the central Mungo River basin. It was described from a small stream in Mambanda, near Kumba.</p><ul><li>Max size: 65 mm SL (2.6 in SL)</li><li>Temperature: 20–26°C (68–79°F)</li><li>pH: 6.0–7.6</li><li>Hardness: 0–5 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/aphyosemion-celiae",
      "html": "<p>Described from a small stream in Mambanda, near Kumba, western Cameroon, and appears restricted to parts of the central Mungo River basin in that region.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/aphyosemion-celiae",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 20–26°C (68–79°F)</li><li>pH: 6.0–7.6</li><li>Hardness: 0–5 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/aphyosemion-celiae",
      "html": "<p>Etymology</p><p>Aphyosemion: from the Greek aphúe, meaning 'a small fish', and semeion, meaning 'banner, flag', in reference to the \"ornamented, often lyre-shaped\" caudal fin in male individuals of genus members.</p><p>celiae: named for Celia Epie of Mambanda, western Cameroon, the eldest daughter of rubber plantation manager John Epie, who hosted Scheel and accompanied him during his collecting trips. The holotype was collected close to Epie's home.</p>"
    }
  ],
